clock-samples.csv   — FIRST, MISLEADING INSTRUMENT. Kept only so the numbers in it are not read as evidence.
                      It sampled three CPUs of the pinned set (4, 12, 70) every 30 s. With 36 sysbench threads
                      on 48 CPUs, an individual core is idle much of the time, so the file cannot tell "this
                      core is parked at 800 MHz" from "the clock is low". Joined against run intervals it
                      appeared to show a median of 1781 MHz inside measured runs, which is an artefact of
                      sampling idle cores, not a measurement of the working clock.

clock-samples2.csv  — the instrument to use. Samples all 48 CPUs of the pinned set (4-27, 60-83) every 30 s and
                      records how many are above 1 GHz, the median and p90 among those, the median over all,
                      and the load average. During a MySQL sysbench run it shows 35-38 cores busy with the busy
                      median steady at about 2900 MHz, so the powersave governor is not a significant noise
                      source while a workload is actually running.

Both files start after 2026-09-08 16:30, so they cover the tail of the MySQL leg, the top-up and the yield
stage, not the legs that finished earlier.
